    "title": "How does the Masters cut line work? A look at how to advance to the weekend",
    "text": "Updated April 9, 2026, 4:24 p.m. ETThe 90th Masters Tournament is past the halfway point of its first round Thursday, but some players are already thinking about what they have to do to play all four rounds.The cut line at the Masters is not a hard and fast number. It changes year to year based on the conditions and how well players navigate them.As Masters Thursday Round 1 scores are posted, here's a look at how the cut line works at Augusta National and what you need to know before Friday evening.How does the Masters cut line work?After two rounds of play, the field is trimmed to the top 50 players and ties. Those players advance to rounds 3 and 4 over the weekend.In past years, there was a rule at the Masters that anyone within 10 shots of the lead also advanced, but that is no longer the case.The Masters cut for 2026: Who's in danger of missing?It's early on Thursday, but scores are trending toward the low side. At 3 p.m., 42 players are at even par of better. Obviously that number will change as the afternoon continues and more holes are played.If the field average is 74, you can expect a cut line of around 4-over 148.The biggest name in danger halfway through Thursday is one of the pre-tournament favorites in Bryson DeChambeau, who was sunk by a triple-bogey on No. 11 and finished at 4-over 76.That means players like Cameron Smith and Charl Schwartzel will have to play as well as today or better to likely be around for the weekend. Five of the six amateurs are also at least 4 over for the tournament and in danger of missing the cut.The Masters cut: What has been the cut number in the past?The cutline changes from year to year, based on the weather and scoring conditions.2025: 3-over 1472024: 7-over 1512023: 4-over 1482022: 5-over 1492021: 4-over 1482020: 1-over 1452019: 4-over 1482018: 6-over 150",
